WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
Men's mental health is something that is super important to talk about and just a really

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.800
important discussion in general, but I think the way that we look at it right now and the way we

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.520
discuss it is counterproductive and honestly really really frustrating to me. I feel like a

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.080
lot of the advocacy surrounding men's mental health is centered around this idea that like

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:18.000
men can't cry and society doesn't accept men for having emotions. And I find this line of

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.800
reasoning a little bit frustrating just because I feel like it suggests that women are accepted by

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.920
society for being emotional and that's just not true. Just because women are more openly emotional

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:28.000
despite scrutiny does not mean that society and the media is like embracing it. The idea that

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.160
women are too emotional is constantly being used to put women down and like say that they're weaker

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.240
and I also think it's just super important to talk about because being emotionally vulnerable

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.560
is not easy and I feel like sometimes it's seen like women have it easier because they're allowed

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:39.440
to be emotional and they're allowed to cry. No, they have the strength to do it anyway despite

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:43.440
scrutiny and to act like it's easier for women is just really frustrating to me. Basically what I'm

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:47.120
saying is the big issue in terms of men's mental health stems from the fact that like men's

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:50.720
relationship and friendships with other men don't leave room for that like emotional connection that

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.760
exists within like female relationships and female friendship. In order to facilitate progress in

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.960
terms of men's mental health I think what needs to be looked at is what's happening within male

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.960
spaces and not necessarily from a societal really broad standpoint because

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:02.360
from that broader standpoint society needs to work on accepting both genders

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.600
and this definitely is not an argument to say that like men's mental health doesn't

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.160
matter and like focusing on men's mental health is sex it's not all we need to

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.440
talk about men's mental health because it's a huge problem but we're looking at it from

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.040
like the bottom down and we need to look at it from down up if that makes sense

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:16.000
obviously I can only speak from the perspective of a woman but I really

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.640
think it's important that we change the conversation regarding men's mental

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.760
health because it is important and it needs to be looked at correctly that's

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:25.880
all she wrote bye guys
